{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please review chess item 2 at exact head 93111cf97d95e8082730c398d87ad80492f3d394 on branch request/chess-item2-host-binding (pushed). This replaces the withdrawn review request fbc749a6, which cited the head assert 04aab534 found defective. Do not merge; I will merge on approval.\n\nGoverning: original request 5682b414 and repair request fbd59f32. Candidates: internal/app ac766341 and docs 80f644e4, both at this head, both resting only on the clean design-note artifact 71c4947b and the repair request. internal/kernel is byte-identical to main 23c4c759, so no kernel candidate is owed -- exported kernel.ReadOpening from the previous head is removed.\n\nWhat changed since 135c4826, and what I would most like challenged:\n\nArchitecture. The affected layers are 4 (application host binding), 5/6 (internal/app as host and boundary adapter) and the kernel, which is unchanged. docs/reference/architecture.md is updated in this same head. The contract question worth your attention: the binding read is deliberately not a verification. It walks the log's commit metadata, authenticates the initializing actor's signature over an intent naming this genesis, and checks the commit tree against the signed payload tree -- but it does not re-verify the sequencer chain, on the reasoning that nothing folds on its authority and the kernel audit runs before any record is folded, so a forged chain loses its projection at the audit rather than through a mis-selected interpreter. If you think a pre-audit read must verify the sequencer signature, note that the walk is oldest-first and sequencer keys rotate, so doing so honestly would need a kernel seam -- which is exactly the scope the repair request told me to avoid unless it is the simplest secure seam. I concluded it is not. Please test that conclusion.\n\nSecurity. Binding authority is the actor key on the log's first record. Unauthorized, unparseable and malformed binding-shaped records are passed over rather than raised, so that nobody able to append can make a repository unreadable by recording one; the payload read is bounded by the genesis payload ceiling. The failure mode I most want a second pair of eyes on is the fallback when the first record does not authenticate: the read returns no binding, which means workroom, rather than an error.\n\nSimplification. The one-entry interpreter map is removed in favour of a value and a one-line lookup, because its newFolder field is spelled in Workroom's own types and no second application could have been registered through it. Selection also moved from eager-at-open to first-use. Both are judgement calls against the literal wording of 5682b414 ('Workroom registered as the default host', 'selects its host at open'), and I would rather you push back now than after a merge.\n\nMeasured on the 5697-commit log here: open about 12ms, selection about 75ms once per workspace. Gates at this exact head: go build ./..., go vet ./..., go test ./... -count=1, all 26 packages ok. Five mutation checks on the new seams all killed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ac7663413e45233605bb59b03fcac540ef02ea9a","branch":"request/chess-item2-host-binding","conditions":"Independent architecture/security/simplification review of exact head 93111cf97d95e8082730c398d87ad80492f3d394 against candidates git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ac7663413e45233605bb59b03fcac540ef02ea9a (internal/app) and git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:80f644e44ab65e6ea1632857254335bc90a2e6f8 (docs), governed by request 5682b414 and repair request fbd59f32. Approval authorizes claude to merge with step-5 succession at the changed paths.","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}